3.3    Proof overview
The first part of Theorem 3 is proven by constructing the state |ψ0 i. For the second part, we
show that the entire state-space H can be expressed as a direct sum of one-dimensional and
two-dimensional subspaces, with each subspace being mapped to itself by U1 and U2 . Each one-
dimensional subspace consists of all multiples of some state |ψi, with U1 |ψi and U2 |ψi being
either |ψi or −|ψi. Therefore, we either have U2 U1 |ψi = |ψi or U2 U1 |ψi = −|ψi. We show
                                                                            ′′
that, if U2 U1 |ψi = |ψi, then |ψi is orthogonal to the starting state |ψstart   i and, therefore, has
no effect on the algorithm.
    For two-dimensional subspaces, we show that each of them has an orthonormal basis |ψ11 i,
|ψ12 i such that U1 |ψ11 i = |ψ11 i and U1 |ψ12 i = −|ψ12 i and another orthonormal basis |ψ21 i, |ψ22 i
such that U2 |ψ21 i = |ψ21 i and U2 |ψ22 i = −|ψ22 i. Then, on this two-dimensional subspace, U2 U1
is a product of two reflections, one w.r.t. |ψ11 i and one w.r.t. |ψ21 i. As in ”two reflections”
analysis [1] of Grover’s search, a product of two reflections in a two-dimensional plane is a
rotation of plane by 2β, where β is the angle between |ψ11 i and |ψ21 i. A rotation of the plane
by 2β has eigenvalues e±iβ . Therefore, we need to lower-bound β.
    Since |ψ21 i and |ψ22 i are orthogonal, the angle between |ψ11 i and |ψ22 i is π2 − β. Therefore,
|hψ22 |ψ11 i| = sin β. Since |ψ22 i belongs to Sx,1 and |ψ11 i belongs to SH,0 , we have

